      "dom_id": "question-68910",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "In a store, there are three different types of cream cheese that come in boxes: \n\n- Box $A$ contains $1$ cup and costs $$1.96\\,.$\n\n- Box $B$ contains $1.2$ cups and costs $$2.40\\,.$\n\n- Box $C$ contains $1.5$ cups and costs $$2.94\\,.$\n\nWhich box of cream cheese is the best deal?",
      "worked_solution": "The best deal is the one with the lowest unit price. So, we need to compute the unit prices for all three boxes. To compute the unit price, we divide the price by the total number of cups.   \n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of cups in box $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n1.96÷1=1.96\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of box $A$ is $$1.96.$\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of cups in box $B,$ we get\n\n$$\n2.40÷1.2=2\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of box $B$ is $$2.00.$\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of cups in box $C,$ we get\n\n$$\n2.94÷1.5=1.96\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of box $C$ is $$1.96$  too.\n\nFrom the above, we see that boxes $A$ and $C$  have the lowest unit price. Therefore, boxes $A$ and  $C$ are the best deal.",
